1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
In Japan, you can pay a
company to vanish overnight.

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:05,615
The firms that do it are called yonige-ya.

3
00:00:05,635 --> 00:00:06,635
Night movers.

4
00:00:06,855 --> 00:00:11,037
Say you lost your job, the debt is choking
you, and you can't face your family.

5
00:00:11,064 --> 00:00:12,115
You make one phone call.

6
00:00:12,135 --> 00:00:15,695
At midnight, a van with its
headlights off pulls up to your door.

7
00:00:15,715 --> 00:00:20,045
A crew slips in quietly and packs
your whole life into a few boxes.

8
00:00:20,065 --> 00:00:21,885
You cancel your phone line, too.

9
00:00:21,915 --> 00:00:22,507
The price?

10
00:00:22,527 --> 00:00:26,745
Around ¥50,000, and up to
¥300,000 for a complicated move.

11
00:00:27,025 --> 00:00:30,855
By morning, you wake up in a one-window
room, in a city you've never seen.

12
00:00:30,875 --> 00:00:33,230
The next day, your mom runs to the police.

13
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:36,553
But if an adult leaves by choice,
and there's no sign of a crime,

14
00:00:36,573 --> 00:00:38,470
police in Japan usually won't search.

15
00:00:38,490 --> 00:00:39,510
The file gets closed.

16
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:41,960
Your mom hires a private detective.

17
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:44,645
Without access to personal
records, he gets nowhere.

18
00:00:44,665 --> 00:00:49,767
In 2025, Japan's police received
over 80,000 missing-person reports,

19
00:00:49,787 --> 00:00:51,615
for all kinds of reasons.

20
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:53,070
Most of them are found.

21
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,555
But some are never heard from again.

22
00:00:55,575 --> 00:00:58,945
The Japanese call people who
choose to vanish jōhatsu.

23
00:00:58,965 --> 00:01:00,115
The evaporated.

24
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:03,165
The detective's report to your
mom says just one sentence.

25
00:01:03,185 --> 00:01:05,930
Your son chose to leave.
